 just these 17 moves okay it's a  pretty decent amount of moves but the  differences between both sides I think  is a great one and soon enough what  we'll see is an additional imbalance  taking place as it stands right now it's  just with regard to the pawn structure  this hanging pawn structure so more  development for for black fully  developed at this point but again not so  harmonious Knight d4 making use of this  pin this Knight is now providing a  disconnect between the Queen and rook so  a very timely Knight d4 is going to  grant this Nitra bishop exchange so the  Queen backs up getting out of the pin  and just for the one moment well just  taking that Bishop right away and after  the recapture we have now one strike in  the center that really is going to turn  the tables these ponds right here the  furthest advanced ones this pawn duo on  the fifth rank is challenged with e4 and  something needs to be done and what  black tries is just pressing forward  which is typical if you do have the  knight you want to try and keep things  closed that d4 move is a step in that  direction it's also a passed pawn but as  will soon see this cupon really could  just never get going and is no serious  threat to white whatsoever additionally  with that advance we now looking a bit  more closely at the pawn structure  recognize that we have a 4-3 majority  in favor in tipping in White's favor on  the Kings side now that said that's not  to say you're supposed to throw up all  of your pawns in this direction it's  more commonly seen that just these F the  F pond and epon for white will be the  ones to press forward and try and induce  some weakness against the black side and  the G and H pawns will just stay back  and act as the shield for that white  King so that's exactly what we see F for  these pawns get rolling Queenie seven  and now II five right here this is now a  static point for black this pawn is  immobile and will soon be pinpointed so  rook to b8 it wasn't really needed  anymore to watch over that c5 square so  now coming back to this b file to exert  pressure on b2 Bishop c4 get  repositioning time at this point placing  this pawn and a pin and looking forward  to an f5 advance where this pond is just  going to strike at a six black gets out  of that and now the Queen's swinging  over again really dictating where the  play will take place in the game so  making use of this whole third rank  right here sweeping from a3 2 H 3 and  now pinpointing that east 6 pawn for a  second time Knight f8 retreat to defend  a6 and now something as simple as b3  makes these rooks look absolutely  ridiculous because there's nothing  that's now going to take place on the  Queen side you can forget about it just  this Bishop and two pawns alone this is  one one thing that I like to do my own  games is just recognize how a particular  grouping of pieces can negate an  opponent's group of pieces and right now  it's just these three highlighted white  pieces negating these three right here  or in other words you could view it as  like a 5 on 5 5 points negating 11  points and there's a on the flip side of  things there's going to now be  a group of let's say these two rooks and  queen and even the bishop that are  influencing the Kings side this bishop  right here really being a multi-purpose  piece in some sense locking down on the  Queen side and still exerting pressure  on that King side so 85 looking to get  in this advance right here looking to  maybe peel up in that a file but things  happen too quickly on the Kings side  f5 something needing to be done about  maybe capturing on e6 or even pressing  forward one more step to f6 and that  King will be in some serious hot water  if that's allowed so the pawn is  captured and it's the rook on F that  recaptures so that this rook can soon  contribute doubling on the F file Knight  h6 r h7 looking to reposition to that g5  square white doubles the Queen backs up  to d8 Queen g3 allows this pot t